#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079251} :activity/id #uuid "e7f6f017-93f9-5ce1-878c-ac54092f9307" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928 :topic/id #uuid "72768bc7-3788-5996-b850-882ff2ef4f6b" :topic/math-academy-id 611} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079643 :tutorial/content "The reciprocals of sine, cosine, and tangent are given special names:\n\n- The reciprocal of **sine** is **cosecant**, abbreviated $\\csc$.\n$\\csc(\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\sin(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}}$\n- The reciprocal of **cosine** is **secant**, abbreviated $\\sec$.\n$\\sec (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\cos(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}}$\n- The reciprocal of **tangent** is **cotangent**, abbreviated $\\cot$.\n$\\cot (\\theta) = \\dfrac 1 {\\tan(\\theta)} = \\dfrac 1 {\\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{adjacent}}} = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}}$\n\nLet's write down expressions for $\\csc(B)$, $\\sec(B)$, and $\\cot(B)$ for the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e9/e98dcc332739ff381e8dc43160a907e471062c5b8a75567ba7dbb8687c008efa.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\csc(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac c b \\\\[5pt] \\sec(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{hypotenuse}} {\\text{adjacent}} = \\dfrac c a \\\\[5pt] \\cot(B) &= \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{opposite}} = \\dfrac a b \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4c546565-505e-5036-a606-7fec250d5073" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2453 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "52fa6b29-a997-555b-b6a1-660efa77d5b2" :step/math-academy-id 10986 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078264 :question/id #uuid "4f967310-913e-5082-ab9b-87b1eceb3ddb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e360e71d-82f6-5226-af82-bfc4d0e5363d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049915}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078951}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratios Given a Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "9b0bf911-fcdf-579f-8c6c-66de2c729bc8" :step/math-academy-id 10987 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079644 :tutorial/content "If we are given two sides of a right triangle, how can we calculate the reciprocal ratios $\\sec$, $\\csc$, or $\\cot$ when we are missing one of the necessary sides in the ratio?\n\nThe trick here is the same that we use for finding $\\sin$, $\\cos$, or $\\tan$: we use the Pythagorean theorem for finding the missing side.\n\nTo illustrate, let's calculate $\\csc L$ in the triangle below.\n\n![](images/9f/9f2e4ea4922783c84a6cb59c5e1532b726d46c9a837a5b1eaa203bbdc6cc1741.png)\n\nRemember that $\\csc$ is the reciprocal of $\\sin$. So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JK}{KL} = \\dfrac{JK}{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JK$, we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJK &= \\sqrt{KL^{2} - JL^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4^{2} - 3^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16 - 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\sin L = \\dfrac{JK}{4} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\csc L &= (\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4})^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7} \\cdot \\sqrt{7}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4\\sqrt{7}}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "c27dd2eb-7f81-577d-805d-4dae042300e6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2455 :tutorial/title "The Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratios and the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "bd643db6-23eb-5063-a803-0b486a1997c2" :step/math-academy-id 10990 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079254}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079254 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078265 :question/id #uuid "d2f40096-4001-5cd9-aeda-52c5894649b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3261"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8659f97-a242-543f-9668-f547ca1f4d95" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048299}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079037}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratio Given a Triangle Using the Pythagorean Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "136b4b27-154f-533e-84ed-203765c9ca7f" :step/math-academy-id 10991}] :activity/title "The Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratios" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078641}]}
